`]})}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Five kernels, five round trips",children:[e.jsx(s.p,{children:"On UC3's H100, a row softmax over a 32,768 × 4,096 bf16 tensor takes 1,248.0 µs when it is written as five PyTorch operations and 253.5 µs in the fastest single kernel we measured, about a fifth of the time (1,248.0 / 253.5 ≈ 4.9). Both compute the same softmax on the same GPU. So why are the five operations 4.9 times slower than one kernel?"}),e.jsx(s.p,{children:"The first part of the answer is where the data goes between the operations. Run eagerly, each of the five operations launches its own kernel, and the profiler counts five kernels per call. A kernel cannot hand its result to the next kernel on the chip. It writes its output tensor to HBM, the GPU's off-chip memory, and the next kernel reads it back. One tensor here is 268 MB, far more than the GPU's on-chip caches hold, so every intermediate makes the full round trip. The single kernel reads the input once, keeps each row on the chip while it computes, and writes the output once."}),e.jsx(s.p,{children:"Softmax does only a few arithmetic operations per byte, so the bytes set its time. Two questions remain: how many bytes each version moves, and whether the byte count accounts for the whole 4.9×."})]})]}),e.jsxs(t,{title:"Orientation",accent:0,number:"2",children:[e.jsxs(s.table,{children:[e.jsx(s.thead,{children:e.jsxs(s.tr,{children:[e.jsx(s.th,{children:"When"}),e.jsx(s.th,{children:"Submission"}),e.jsx(s.th,{children:"Reflection questions"})]})}),e.jsx(s.tbody,{children:e.jsxs(s.tr,{children:[e.jsx(s.td,{children:"After Week 5, before Week 6"}),e.jsxs(s.td,{children:["None (",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"scripts/kernels/"})," optional)"]}),e.jsx(s.td,{children:"Three, discussed in the Week 6 tutorial"})]})})]}),e.jsxs(s.ul,{children:[`
