`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Two ideas to bring from Weeks 4 and 5",children:[e.jsx(s.p,{children:"This chapter is required reading between Week 5 and Week 6, with no submission. It ends with three reflection questions, which the Week 6 tutorial discusses. Prepare a short answer to each. The question slides along the way are self-checks: think about the question first, then reveal the answer."}),e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["Two earlier ideas carry the chapter. From Week 4, the roofline: a kernel's time obeys ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"T ≥ max(F/P_peak, B/β)"}),", so an operation with few FLOPs per byte of HBM traffic is limited by the bytes it moves. From Week 5, mixed precision: tensors are stored in bf16, with 8 exponent bits and 7 mantissa bits, while sensitive reductions such as sums are accumulated in fp32."]}),e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["The folder ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"scripts/kernels/"})," holds the kernel this chapter walks through, a correctness check and a benchmark. Running them is optional and needs an NVIDIA GPU with the CuTe DSL installed. We ran them on UC3's H100 for the measurements given here. Reading and explaining a kernel is the goal. Writing a production kernel from a blank file is a different skill."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(t,{title:"A kernel is one function run by thousands of threads",accent:3,number:"3",items:"4",children:[e.jsxs(s.ul,{children:[`
