`,e.jsxs(s.li,{children:[e.jsx(s.code,{children:"torch.softmax"}),": one kernel per call"]}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Kernels, launches and where a custom kernel can help",children:[e.jsx(s.p,{children:"A GPU kernel is a function that runs on the GPU. The host program on the CPU launches it over a grid of threads, and every thread runs the same function, using its own index to choose its data. One launch starts thousands of threads."}),e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["An operation on CUDA tensors in PyTorch queues one or more kernel launches. An operation that only changes metadata, such as a view, launches none. On UC3's H100, for a softmax over a 32,768 × 4,096 bf16 tensor, the PyTorch profiler counted one kernel per call of ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"torch.softmax"}),", and five for the same softmax written as five separate operations."]}),e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["Standard calls such as ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"torch.softmax"})," and matrix multiplication already run optimized kernels, so rewriting one of them in a lower-level language rarely helps by itself. The opportunity is usually a composition boundary: several operations launch separately, and each writes an intermediate tensor to HBM that the next reads back, although the data could have stayed on the chip. Week 4 showed that FlashAttention wins by never writing the attention score and probability matrices to HBM ",e.jsx(i,{id:"dao2022flashattention",children:"Dao et al., 2022"}),". The row softmax is a simpler operation with the same kind of boundary."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(t,{title:"Five operations move four times the bytes of one",accent:4,number:"4",children:[e.jsx(a,{math:"\\text{traffic ratio} \\;=\\; \\frac{8 \\text{ passes (decomposed)}}{2 \\text{ passes (fused)}} \\;=\\; 4\\times",label:"(1)"}),e.jsxs(s.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(s.li,{children:"Eager chain: 5 reads, 3 writes"}),`
`,e.jsx(s.li,{children:"Fused: 1 read, 1 write"}),`
