`]}),e.jsxs(s.table,{children:[e.jsx(s.thead,{children:e.jsxs(s.tr,{children:[e.jsx(s.th,{children:"UC3's H100, 32,768 × 4,096 bf16"}),e.jsx(s.th,{children:"Time"}),e.jsx(s.th,{children:"Kernels"})]})}),e.jsxs(s.tbody,{children:[e.jsxs(s.tr,{children:[e.jsx(s.td,{children:"Eager chain"}),e.jsx(s.td,{children:"1,248.0 µs"}),e.jsx(s.td,{children:"5"})]}),e.jsxs(s.tr,{children:[e.jsx(s.td,{children:e.jsx(s.code,{children:"torch.softmax"})}),e.jsx(s.td,{children:"466.8 µs"}),e.jsx(s.td,{children:"1"})]}),e.jsxs(s.tr,{children:[e.jsxs(s.td,{children:["Fastest fused kernel (course ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"512x8"}),")"]}),e.jsx(s.td,{children:"253.5 µs"}),e.jsx(s.td,{children:"1"})]})]})]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Counting passes, and when the ratio can exceed 4",children:[e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["A pass is one full read or one full write of the tensor in HBM. Write a row softmax as five operations: the maximum ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"m = amax(x)"}),", the shift ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"x − m"}),", the exponential, the sum ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"s"}),", and the division by ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"s"}),". Run eagerly, each is its own kernel. ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"amax"})," reads ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"x"}),". The subtraction reads ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"x"})," and writes ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"x − m"}),". The exponential reads that and writes ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"exp(x − m)"}),". The sum reads the exponentials, and the division reads them again and writes the output. That is five reads and three writes, eight passes. (The maximum and the sum also write one value per row, which is negligible.) A fused kernel reads ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"x"})," once and writes the output once, two passes. Formula (1) divides the two counts."]}),e.jsx(s.p,{children:"When does fusion save a factor of 4 in time? Assume that every intermediate goes to HBM and back, which holds here because one tensor is 268 MB and the L2 cache is far smaller, that every pass runs at the same bandwidth, and that nothing else costs time. Then each version's time is its pass count times the time of one pass, and the ratio is 8 / 2 = 4. Relaxing the assumptions moves the ratio in known directions. Intermediates that stay in L2 make the eager chain faster, and arithmetic time, which both versions pay, dilutes the ratio, so both lower it. Extra kernel launches and unequal bandwidth raise it."}),e.jsx(s.p,{children:"The table gives medians from UC3's H100 for a 32,768 × 4,096 bf16 input (slide 25 has the full table and the protocol). The eager chain is 1,248.0 / 253.5 ≈ 4.9 times slower than the fastest fused kernel. Launches do not explain the difference: the device time of one profiled eager call, 1,257.4 µs, is close to its median, so little time passes between its kernels. Bandwidth does. If the eager chain moves the eight passes counted above, 8 × 268 MB ≈ 2.15 GB in 1,248.0 µs, it averages about 1,721 GB/s, 81% of the 2,132.9 GB/s that a plain copy reaches on this card. The fused kernel moves its two passes at 2,118.0 GB/s, 99.3% of the copy. The byte ratio times the bandwidth ratio gives the measured gap: 4 × 2,118.0 / 1,721 ≈ 4.9."}),e.jsxs(s.p,{children:[e.jsx(s.code,{children:"torch.softmax"})," is itself one fused kernel with one read and one write, but it reaches only 53.9% of the copy's bandwidth, so the eager chain is only 1,248.0 / 466.8 ≈ 2.7 times slower than it. Moving fewer bytes and moving them at full speed are separate properties. The traffic count tells you what fusion can remove, and a benchmark what a kernel achieves."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(t,{title:"Softmax falls far below every ridge",accent:3,figure:e.jsx(k,{focus:"softmax"}),figureWidth:"3/5",number:"5",children:[e.jsxs(s.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(s.li,{children:"About 1.25 FLOP/byte: 5 operations, 4 bytes"}),`
`,e.jsx(s.li,{children:"Drawn roof: bf16 Tensor Core peak, ridge 295"}),`
`,e.jsx(s.li,{children:"fp32 roof: about 15× lower, ridge 20"}),`
`,e.jsx(s.li,{children:"Chart marker: teaching value 2"}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Softmax's intensity, its roof, and when fusion does not pay",children:[e.jsx(s.p,{children:"Per element, a read-once, write-once softmax performs about five operations (a comparison for the maximum, a subtraction, an exponential, an addition for the sum, a multiplication by the reciprocal) and moves 4 bytes, 2 read and 2 written in bf16. That is about 1.25 FLOP per byte. It is an estimate, because the exponential runs on the special-function unit (SFU) and FLOP conventions differ. The chart marks softmax at 2 FLOP/byte, a teaching value it shares with Week 4."}),e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["The roof drawn in the chart is Week 4's: the H100 SXM's dense bf16 Tensor Core peak of 989.5 TFLOP/s, with its ridge at about 295 FLOP/byte. Softmax has no matrix product, so it cannot use Tensor Cores. Its own roof is the fp32 peak of 67 TFLOP/s ",e.jsx(i,{id:"nvidia2022h100datasheet",children:"NVIDIA, 2022"}),", about 15 times lower, with a ridge at 67 / 3.35 = 20 FLOP/byte. The marker lies left of both ridges. For the tensor of slide 4, our own arithmetic gives at least 5 × 134 × 10⁶ / (67 × 10¹²) ≈ 10 µs for the arithmetic and at least 160 µs for moving 268 MB in and 268 MB out at 3.35 TB/s. Removing full-tensor passes is the lever."]}),e.jsx(s.p,{children:"Fusion does not always pay. A small tensor can stay in the L2 cache (50 MB on the H100 SXM), so its intermediates never reach HBM, the traffic count overstates the saving, and launch overhead dominates. A compute-bound operation, such as a large matrix multiplication, is limited by its arithmetic, and removing bytes changes little. A chain of launches in a profile is only a candidate. The case is strong when the chain is memory-bound, its intermediates reach HBM, and the compiler leaves the boundary in place."})]})]}),e.jsxs(t,{title:"Try the library and the compiler before writing a kernel",accent:6,number:"6",items:"5",children:[e.jsxs(s.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(s.li,{children:["Path: library → ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"torch.compile"})," → profiler → custom kernel"]}),`
`,e.jsx(s.li,{children:"Compiler: fuses pointwise and reduction chains"}),`
`,e.jsxs(s.li,{children:["Picks GEMM backends, one in the CuTe DSL ",e.jsx(i,{id:"patel2026cutedslgemm",children:"Patel et al., 2026"})]}),`
`,e.jsx(s.li,{children:"Custom kernel: only for a profiled boundary"}),`
`,e.jsx(s.li,{children:"Done: correctness check, then fair benchmark"}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"The escalation path and what ends it",children:[e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["Start with the library implementation where one exists: it is already tuned and covers many shapes and dtypes. Profile the eager program to find a candidate, a chain of launches whose intermediates travel through HBM. Then try ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"torch.compile"}),". Its default backend, TorchInductor, fuses chains of pointwise and reduction operations. For matrix multiplications it can choose among specialized GEMM backends, one of them written in the CuTe DSL ",e.jsx(i,{id:"patel2026cutedslgemm",children:"Patel et al., 2026"}),". On UC3's H100, ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"torch.compile"})," turned the five-kernel eager softmax into one kernel (slide 25). Profile the compiled program again and see what remains. Write a custom kernel only for a boundary that is still there, and only when the time it saves justifies maintaining it. FlashAttention-4 is a kernel designed together with its algorithm for one hardware imbalance ",e.jsx(i,{id:"zadouri2026flashattention4",children:"Zadouri et al., 2026"}),"."]}),e.jsx(s.p,{children:"A custom kernel starts from a hypothesis, which boundary it removes and how many bytes that saves, and ends with two pieces of evidence. The first is a correctness check against a trusted reference, run before any timing. The second is a benchmark that gives the kernel and its comparators the same shapes, dtypes, outputs, synchronization and measurement protocol. A faster time on a different shape, dtype or looser output is no win."})]})]})]}),`
