`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Inside one SM",children:[e.jsx(s.p,{children:"Week 4's figure showed an H100 as 132 streaming multiprocessors (SMs) around a shared L2 cache and off-chip HBM. The same figure opens here on one SM, and its GPU button returns to the whole chip."}),e.jsx(s.p,{children:"A few terms first. A thread runs the kernel's code. A warp is a group of 32 threads that execute together, and each thread is one lane of its warp. A cooperative thread array (CTA) is a group of up to 1,024 threads that run on one SM and share memory. Slides 9 to 11 return to each of these levels."}),e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["Each SM is partitioned into four sub-partitions. Each has a warp scheduler, a register file and its own execution units: integer and floating-point cores, load/store units, a special-function unit (SFU) and a Tensor Core. The four share the SM's L1 data cache and shared memory and its texture units ",e.jsx(i,{id:"nvidia2026nsight",children:"NVIDIA, 2026b"}),". On the H100 a sub-partition has 32 FP32 cores, 16 INT32 cores, 16 FP64 cores and one Tensor Core, a quarter of the SM's 128, 64, 64 and 4 ",e.jsx(i,{id:"nvidia2022hopper",children:"Andersch et al., 2022"}),". Its register file holds 16,384 32-bit registers, 64 KB ",e.jsx(i,{id:"nvidia2022hopper",children:"Andersch et al., 2022"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["A warp is allocated to one sub-partition and stays there from launch to completion ",e.jsx(i,{id:"nvidia2026nsight",children:"NVIDIA, 2026b"}),". Its threads' registers live in that sub-partition's register file. An SM holds at most 64 warps at a time (slide 9), so, by our own count, each scheduler has at most 16 to choose from. On every cycle, each scheduler picks one warp that is ready to issue and issues its next instruction or instructions ",e.jsx(i,{id:"nvidia2026nsight",children:"NVIDIA, 2026b"}),'. A warp that waits for data from memory is not ready, so in the meantime the scheduler issues from another warp. The slide "Bytes in flight hide memory latency" builds on this.']}),e.jsx(s.p,{children:"The softmax kernel uses only some of these units. Its loads and stores go through the load/store units, the maximum, the subtraction, the sum and the scaling run on the floating-point cores, and the exponential runs on the special-function unit (slide 23). It has no matrix product, so it never uses a Tensor Core, and the bf16 Tensor Core roof of slide 5 is not its roof. The Tensor Memory Accelerator (TMA) and the warpgroups (groups of four warps) that FlashAttention uses come in the To FlashAttention tab."})]})]}),e.jsxs(t,{title:"Data lives in four tiers, from small and fast to large and slow",accent:2,number:"8",children:[e.jsx(c,{}),e.jsxs(s.table,{children:[e.jsx(s.thead,{children:e.jsxs(s.tr,{children:[e.jsx(s.th,{children:"Tier"}),e.jsx(s.th,{children:"H100 SXM size"}),e.jsx(s.th,{children:"Shared by"}),e.jsx(s.th,{children:"Rough latency"})]})}),e.jsxs(s.tbody,{children:[e.jsxs(s.tr,{children:[e.jsx(s.td,{children:"Registers"}),e.jsx(s.td,{children:"256 KB per SM (64K × 32 bit)"}),e.jsx(s.td,{children:"one thread"}),e.jsx(s.td,{children:"lowest"})]}),e.jsxs(s.tr,{children:[e.jsx(s.td,{children:"Shared memory"}),e.jsx(s.td,{children:"up to 228 KB per SM"}),e.jsx(s.td,{children:"one CTA"}),e.jsx(s.td,{children:"about 10–20 ns"})]}),e.jsxs(s.tr,{children:[e.jsx(s.td,{children:"L2 cache"}),e.jsx(s.td,{children:"50 MB"}),e.jsx(s.td,{children:"all SMs"}),e.jsx(s.td,{children:"about 150–200 ns"})]}),e.jsxs(s.tr,{children:[e.jsx(s.td,{children:"HBM"}),e.jsx(s.td,{children:"80 GB at 3.35 TB/s"}),e.jsx(s.td,{children:"all SMs"}),e.jsx(s.td,{children:"about 400 ns"})]})]})]}),e.jsxs(s.ul,{children:[`
