`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Residency: how many CTAs fit on one SM",children:[e.jsx(s.p,{children:"A CTA, also called a thread block, runs on one SM from start to finish. Its threads share the CTA's shared memory and can wait for each other at a CTA-wide barrier, and the softmax reduction uses both. A grid is all the CTAs of one launch. The hardware places CTAs on SMs as resources become free, so a grid can be far larger than what fits on the GPU at once."}),e.jsx(s.p,{children:"How many CTAs fit on one SM at once, the residency, is set by the tightest limit. The course kernel's 1,024 threads per CTA allow two CTAs under the limit of 2,048 threads per SM. Registers agree: the compiled kernel uses 32 registers per thread, and Formula (2) shows that two CTAs then fill the SM's 64K registers. At 33 or more registers per thread only one CTA would fit. Shared memory does not limit this kernel, which uses 32 fp32 values, 128 bytes, per CTA. Two CTAs of 32 warps also reach the limit of 64 warps per SM."}),e.jsx(s.p,{children:"The softmax of slide 4 launches one CTA per row, 32,768 in all. With 132 SMs holding two each, 264 run at a time, and 32,768 / 264 ≈ 124.1, so the GPU works through the grid in 125 rounds. Launch overhead matters when a program launches many tiny grids. For one large memory-bound grid, the bytes dominate."})]})]}),e.jsxs(t,{title:"Bytes in flight hide memory latency",accent:7,number:"12",children:[e.jsx(a,{math:"\\text{bytes in flight} = \\text{bandwidth} \\times \\text{latency}",label:"(3)"}),e.jsx(a,{math:"3.35\\ \\text{TB/s} \\times 400\\ \\text{ns} \\approx 1.3\\ \\text{MB} \\approx 10\\ \\text{KB per SM}",label:"(4)"}),e.jsxs(s.ul,{children:[`
