`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"How we measured, and what each comparison shows",children:[e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["The measurement ran on UC3's H100, which ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"nvidia-smi"}),' reports as "NVIDIA H100" with 95,830 MiB, with driver 615.71.09, PyTorch 2.13.0 built for CUDA 13.0, ',e.jsx(s.code,{children:"nvidia-cutlass-dsl"})," 4.8.0 and QuACK 0.6.5, on 2026-10-10. The input is a 32,768 × 4,096 bf16 tensor, 268 MB, and every row of the table takes bf16 input and returns bf16 output."]}),e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["Correctness came first: all three course variants passed the check of slide 23 for 1, 8, 1,000 and 1,024 rows. The benchmark rotates through four 268 MB input buffers, 1,074 MB in total, far more than the card's 60 MiB L2. CUDA events time a loop of 100 back-to-back calls after 10 warm-up calls, the loop runs five times, and the table gives the median time per call. The spread is the range of the five loop times, as a percentage of the median. Effective bandwidth divides the same ideal byte count, one read and one write (2 × 268 MB), by the median for every row ",e.jsx(i,{id:"nvidia2024bestpractices",children:"NVIDIA, 2024a"}),', so a row that moves extra bytes shows a lower number. "% of copy" compares with the copy row, the practical ceiling on this card, and "% of peak" with the theoretical 2,395.9 GB/s of slide 8. The PyTorch profiler counted the kernels of one call. The copy is a memory copy and launches no kernel. As a cross-check, the summed device time of a single profiled call agrees with the medians within 12% (242.9 µs for the copy, 243.5 µs for ',e.jsx(s.code,{children:"512x8"}),")."]}),e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["The GPU's clock was not constant. During most rows the driver reported a software power cap (throttle reason 0x4), and the SM clock read right after each row ranged from 1,545 MHz, after ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"torch.softmax"}),", to the maximum of 1,980 MHz, after the copy and QuACK. Each reading is one sample. They show that rows ran at different SM clocks, not how much each row was slowed."]}),e.jsxs(s.p,{children:[e.jsx(s.code,{children:"torch.compile"})," turned five kernels into one, at 359.3 µs, 70.1% of the copy and 1,248.0 / 359.3 ≈ 3.5 times faster than the eager chain. Without any kernel code, it was slightly faster than the course's walkthrough kernel at 369.4 µs. This is why slide 6 tries the compiler first."]}),e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["The layout comparison is not resolved by this run. ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"1024x4"})," and ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"strided"})," differ only in the layout of slide 16: one 8-byte load per thread against four 2-byte loads, both coalesced. Their medians, 369.4 and 377.6 µs, differ by about 2%, less than the strided row's 5.6% spread. ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"torch.softmax"}),", which uses the strided mapping, had a 24.3% spread and the lowest clock reading, so its median of 466.8 µs supports no argument about layout."]}),e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["The two kernels with 16-byte loads reach the copy's speed. ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"512x8"})," takes 253.5 µs, 99.3% of the copy, against 369.4 µs and 68.1% for ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"1024x4"}),". The compiled code of all three course variants uses 32 registers per thread, so four ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"512x8"})," CTAs of 512 threads fit on an SM, against two ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"1024x4"})," CTAs of 1,024: both hold 2,048 threads, 64 warps, the same occupancy. Each ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"512x8"})," thread loads 16 bytes in one instruction, so when its CTAs load at once the SM has 2,048 × 16 bytes = 32 KB in flight, against 16 KB for ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"1024x4"}),", the second lever of slide 12. The variant also changes a second thing: each CTA has 16 warps instead of 32, so each cross-warp fold combines 16 partials. This run does not separate the two effects. QuACK reaches the same speed, 254.5 µs and 98.9%, with the different design of slide 19: two rows per 128-thread CTA, 16-byte asynchronous copies through shared memory and the online softmax."]}),e.jsx(s.p,{children:"The table measures one shape, one dtype and one GPU. Another setting needs its own measurement."})]})]})]}),`
