`]})]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Two tiles by hand",children:[e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["One query row meets four keys in two tiles, K/V blocks of two keys each. Tile 1 holds the scores 1 and 2 with the values 1 and 0, tile 2 the scores 3 and 0 with the values 2 and 1. Each value is a single number here, so ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"o"})," is a single number too. What are ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"m"}),", ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"l"})," and ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"o"})," after each tile?"]}),e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["Start with ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"m = −∞"}),", ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"l = 0"})," and ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"o = 0"}),". Tile 1 raises the maximum to ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"m = 2"}),". The old ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"l"})," and ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"o"})," are zero, so the rescale changes nothing, and the tile adds its own terms: ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"l = e^(1−2) + e^(2−2) = e^(−1) + 1 ≈ 1.368"})," and ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"o = e^(−1) · 1 + 1 · 0 ≈ 0.368"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["Tile 2 holds the score 3, which raises the maximum to ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"m = 3"}),". Both old sums were computed against the maximum 2, so Formula (8) first multiplies them by ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"e^(2−3) = e^(−1) ≈ 0.368"}),". Then the tile adds its terms against the new maximum: ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"l ≈ 0.368 · 1.368 + e^(3−3) + e^(0−3) ≈ 0.503 + 1 + 0.050 ≈ 1.553"})," and ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"o ≈ 0.368 · 0.368 + 1 · 2 + e^(−3) · 1 ≈ 0.135 + 2 + 0.050 ≈ 2.185"}),". Formula (9) divides once at the end: ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"o / l ≈ 2.185 / 1.553 ≈ 1.407"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["Check it against the softmax over all four scores at once. With the maximum 3, the exponentials of 1, 2, 3 and 0 are ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"e^(−2)"}),", ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"e^(−1)"}),", 1 and ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"e^(−3)"}),", which sum to about 1.553, and weighting the values 1, 0, 2 and 1 by them gives about 2.185. The ratio is the same 1.407. The agreement is exact: the rescale turned tile 1's ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"e^(1−2)"})," into ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"e^(1−2) · e^(2−3) = e^(1−3)"}),", the term the softmax over all four scores at once computes. The probabilities themselves, about 0.087, 0.237, 0.644 and 0.032, were never formed."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(t,{title:"FlashAttention adds Tensor Cores and an asynchronous pipeline",accent:4,number:"28",items:"5",children:[e.jsxs(s.ul,{children:[`
