`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"A guide for optional source reading",children:[e.jsx(s.p,{children:"To go further, label each block of a production kernel with one of four roles: layout (which thread owns which data), partition (how the tensor is cut into tiles and slices), reduction or MMA (the arithmetic) and pipeline (how data moves between tiers and overlaps with compute). Recognizing the roles is enough."}),e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["In QuACK's softmax, ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"_threads_per_row"})," picks 64 threads for a 4,096-wide row, and CTAs have 128 threads for rows up to 16K wide, so one CTA handles two rows. The vector width is 128 bits divided by the element width, eight bf16 values. ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"row_reduce"})," in ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"reduce.py"})," combines the thread, warp and CTA tiers of slide 22. Very wide rows are split across a cluster of CTAs, which for 16-bit rows starts above 16K elements, so at 4,096 the cluster has one CTA ",e.jsx(i,{id:"daoailab2026quack",children:"Dao-AILab, 2026"}),". In FlashAttention-4, find the tile layouts, the MMA instructions, the softmax with its conditional rescaling, and the producer and consumer warps ",e.jsx(i,{id:"zadouri2026flashattention4",children:"Zadouri et al., 2026"}),"."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(t,{title:"A quarter of the bytes, moved 1.23 times faster",question:"So why are five PyTorch ops 4.9× slower than one kernel?",accent:2,number:"31",promptitems:"0",children:[e.jsxs(x,{children:[e.jsx(a,{math:"\\frac{8\\ \\text{passes}}{2\\ \\text{passes}} \\times \\frac{2{,}118.0\\ \\text{GB/s}}{1{,}721\\ \\text{GB/s}} \\approx 4.9"}),e.jsxs(s.ul,{children:[`
