`]})]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"The answer in two factors",children:[e.jsx(s.p,{children:"So why are five PyTorch operations 4.9 times slower than one kernel on the same GPU? The gap has two factors: how many bytes each version moves, and how fast it moves them."}),e.jsx(s.p,{children:"The first factor is the bytes. Run eagerly, the five operations are five kernels, and each intermediate tensor goes to HBM and back. That is five full reads and three full writes, eight passes of 268 MB, against one read and one write for a fused kernel (slide 4). Softmax performs about 1.25 FLOP per byte, far left of every ridge, so its time follows the bytes it moves (slide 5). A quarter of the bytes accounts for a factor of 4."}),e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["The second factor is the bandwidth each version reaches. Suppose the eager chain moves its eight passes, about 2.15 GB, in 1,248.0 µs. It then averages about 1,721 GB/s, 81% of the 2,132.9 GB/s that a plain copy reaches on UC3's card. The fastest fused kernel, ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"512x8"}),", moves its two passes at 2,118.0 GB/s, 99.3% of the copy. The bandwidth ratio is 2,118.0 / 1,721 ≈ 1.23, and the two factors multiply: 4 × 1.23 ≈ 4.9, the measured gap."]}),e.jsx(s.p,{children:"A fused kernel removes the intermediate passes because its data stays on the chip. In the course kernel one CTA handles one row. It keeps the row in fp32 registers through both sweeps, the maximum and the exponentials with their sum, and uses shared memory only for the 32 warp partials, so the row is read once and written once (slide 19)."}),e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["Fusion alone does not reach the copy's speed. ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"torch.softmax"})," and the walkthrough kernel ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"1024x4"})," are single kernels too, yet they reach 53.9% and 68.1% of the copy. HBM delivers its bandwidth only when enough bytes are in flight to cover its latency (slide 12). Each ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"512x8"})," thread loads 16 bytes in one instruction, so its SM can have 32 KB in flight against 16 KB for ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"1024x4"}),", at the same occupancy, and it reaches 99.3% of the copy. The variant also halves the warps per CTA, and our run does not separate the two effects. QuACK reaches 98.9% with its own 16-byte design (slide 25)."]}),e.jsxs(s.p,{children:["Removing the passes does not require writing a kernel first. ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"torch.compile"})," turned the five eager kernels into one, 3.5 times faster than the eager chain, and a custom kernel is worth its upkeep only for a boundary the compiler leaves in place (slide 6)."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(t,{title:"Reflection questions",accent:5,number:"32",items:"4",children:[e.jsx(s.p,{children:"No submission. Bring short answers to the Week 6 tutorial."}),e.jsxs(s.ol,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(s.li,{children:["An eager RMSNorm in bf16 squares ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"x"}),", takes each row's mean, applies ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"rsqrt"}),", multiplies ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"x"})," by the result and then by the weight ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"g"}),", one kernel per step. Count its full-tensor passes against a fused kernel and state the ceiling with its assumptions. The course's ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"RMSNorm"})," upcasts ",e.jsx(s.code,{children:"x"})," to fp32 before these steps and casts the result back. How does that change the count and the ceiling?"]}),`
